{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-341717",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{x}\\cdot (x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1) \\\\  & ={x}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot (x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
